Here is the full **testsmells.org 19-smell academic catalog** audit of `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`, with integration-test calibration applied.

---

## Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 3           | 3              |
| Medium   | 2           | 2              |
| Low      | 0           | 0              |
| **Total**| **5**       | **5** (of 8)   |

The remaining 14 smells from the catalog (**Sensitive Equality**, **Exception Handling**, **General Fixture**, **Ignored Test**, **Duplicate Assert**, **Lazy Test**, **Constructor Initialization**, **Default Test**, **Redundant Print**, **Redundant Assertion**, **Resource Optimism**, **Empty Test**, **Mystery Guest**, **Eager Test**) are **clean**. Mystery Guest and Eager Test were evaluated but downgraded to not-an-issue — the in-memory SQLite is hermetic, and multi-method calls are expected in integration tests.

---

## High Severity

### 1. Assertion-Free Test (a.k.a. "Unknown Test")

**Location:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (line 113–119)
**Problem:** Executes 100 inserts but asserts nothing. The test passes even if every insert silently fails or corrupts data.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — no assertion, false confidence
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
}

// ✅ Fix — verify the inserts actually persisted
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count);
}
```

### 2. Sleepy Test

**Location:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 87)
**Problem:** `Thread.Sleep(3000)` adds 3 seconds of wall-clock delay, is non-deterministic (may be too short on slow CI, unnecessarily long otherwise), and blocks the thread despite the method being `async Task`.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — blocking sleep in an async method
Thread.Sleep(3000);
Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));

// ✅ Fix — poll with timeout instead of sleeping a fixed duration
var deadline = DateTime.UtcNow.AddSeconds(5);
while (!_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
{
    if (DateTime.UtcNow > deadline)
        Assert.Fail("Notification was not sent within timeout.");
    await Task.Delay(100);
}
```

> **Integration calibration:** Sleepy Test remains High even in integration suites per testsmells.org guidance — a polling/retry loop is always preferable to a fixed sleep.

### 3. Conditional Test Logic

**Location:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (lines 101–108)
**Problem:** The `if/else` branch means only one set of assertions runs per execution. If `loaded` is never a `PremiumUser`, the discount-rate assertion is dead code. The test silently verifies a weaker property than intended.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — one branch is never tested
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
else
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);

// ✅ Fix — split into two explicit tests
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_StandardUser_ReturnsCorrectName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");
    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_PremiumUser_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("vip@example.com", "VIP", discountRate: 0.1m));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("vip@example.com") as PremiumUser;
    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

---

## Medium Severity

### 4. Assertion Roulette

**Location:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (lines 35–38)
**Problem:** 4 consecutive assertions with no message parameters. When one fails, the MSTest output shows only `Assert.AreEqual failed` — you must read the source to determine which property was wrong.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — 4 bare assertions
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);

// ✅ Fix — add business-context messages
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should survive round-trip");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should survive round-trip");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Database should assign a positive ID");
```

### 5. Magic Number Test

**Location:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 87)
**Problem:** `Thread.Sleep(3000)` — why 3000ms? The value is unexplained. Is it a known SLA? A guess?

```csharp
// ✅ Fix — name the constant
private const int NotificationTimeoutMs = 3000;
```

---

## Smell-Free Patterns ✅

These tests are well-designed — worth highlighting as examples for the team:

| Test | What's Good |
|------|-------------|
| `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` | Clear AAA structure, verifies multiple properties of the persisted entity |
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Focused: one mutation, one verification |
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Asserts absence (`IsNull`) — correct negative check |
| `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` | Uses `CollectionAssert` — appropriate API choice |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Properly `await`s `Task.WhenAll`; tests a real concurrency concern |
| **Class-level design** | `sealed` class, `[TestInitialize]`/`[TestCleanup]` lifecycle, hermetic in-memory SQLite, `[TestCategory("Integration")]` marker |

---

## Prioritized Remediation

| Priority | Smell | Fix Effort | Impact |
|----------|-------|-----------|--------|
| 1 | Assertion-Free Test | Low — add 2 lines | Eliminates false confidence |
| 2 | Conditional Test Logic | Medium — split into 2 tests | Eliminates dead assertion path |
| 3 | Sleepy Test | Medium — replace with poll loop | Removes 3s delay + flakiness risk |
| 4 | Assertion Roulette | Low — add message strings | Improves failure diagnostics |
| 5 | Magic Number Test | Low — extract constant | Improves readability |

**Bottom line:** 5 of 8 tests are clean and well-structured. The 3 High findings are the priority — they each risk hiding real bugs. The 2 Medium findings are diagnostic/readability improvements to tackle opportunistically.
